{
  "_meta": {
    "description": "Wright's Law learning rates per technology. Learning rate = fraction of cost reduction per doubling of cumulative installed capacity. Includes Lafond-style log-normal forecast σ for distributional projection. Used by Layer 2 to project crossover years and stranded-asset triggers.",
    "sources": [
      "Way, Ives, Mealy, Farmer (2022). 'Empirically grounded technology forecasts and the energy transition.' Joule 6(9). https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joule.2022.08.009",
      "Lafond, Bailey, Bakker, Rebois, Zadourian, McSharry, Farmer (2018). 'How well do experience curves predict technological progress?' Tech Forecasting & Social Change 128: 104-117. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.techfore.2017.11.001",
      "Santa Fe Institute Performance Curve Database: https://pcdb.santafe.edu/",
      "IRENA (2023). 'Renewable Power Generation Costs in 2022.' https://www.irena.org/publications/2023/Aug/Renewable-Power-Generation-Costs-in-2022",
      "Ziegler & Trancik (2021). 'Re-examining rates of lithium-ion battery technology improvement and cost decline.' Energy & Environ Sci 14: 1635-1651."
    ],
    "method": "Cost(Q) = Cost(Q0) * (Q/Q0)^(log2(1-LR)). LR = learning rate; Q = cumulative production. Lafond σ enables distributional forecasts: log(Cost_t) ~ Normal(log(Cost_pred_t), σ * sqrt(t))."
  },
